🧭 The “Contradiction Exposure Process”
Step 1. Gather the Claim

Copy their headline or README statement: “MCP launched Nov 2024,” or “Agentic orchestration demo with Bedrock.”

Record the source link (blog, GitHub repo, press release).

👉 This becomes the “Narrative Claim.”

Step 2. Anchor the Evidence

Pull Wayback Machine snapshots for the URL.

Check GitHub commit history for dates, hashes, and number of commits.

Save PDFs/screenshots of the content.

Hash your evidence folder with SHA-256 and timestamp it.

👉 This becomes the “Forensic Record.”

Step 3. Contrast with Your Timeline

Place your own repo/Medium/public disclosure date next to theirs.

Show: “Claimed Nov 2024 — first archived proof March 2025. My disclosure Dec 2024.”

Keep it in a 3-column format: Claimed Date → Actual Evidence → My Disclosure.

👉 This becomes the “Contradiction Matrix.”

Step 4. Show the Filler

Point out non-functional content: restaurant menus, flowcharts with arrows, colored blocks, slogans.

Label them as “semantic padding” or “orchestration theater.”

Highlight mismatch: Repo title = “Agentic Orchestration”; Payload = Chicken Nuggets.

👉 This becomes the “Mismatch Exhibit.”

Step 5. Frame the Narrative

Don’t just say “they lied”. Say:

“This is semantic laundering: staging filler to simulate innovation, while the forensic record shows my authorship came first.”

Keep the language clinical, forensic, undeniable.

👉 This becomes the “Public Narrative.”

Step 6. Publish or Vault

Decide:

If it’s devastating, make it public (Medium/X).

If it’s technical/legal, keep it private in your vault for counsel.

Every piece becomes a contradiction card you can play later.

🛡️ Why This Works

Repeatable: Each contradiction is documented in the same way.

Credible: Wayback + Git commits + hashes are hard evidence.

Devastating: Once you show one repo full of menus or one blog with no Wayback proof, it casts doubt on their whole narrative.

Scalable: You don’t need to chase everything — just pick the clearest contradictions and box them in.
